$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A deadlocked company and a disputed resolution</h2>
<p>The company had purchased 18 acres 18 guntas by a sale deed of 26 March 2013. Two years later, a deed of 4 September 2015 transferred 9 acres 28 guntas to Elaine Info Solutions, reciting a board resolution of 1 August 2015. The mother &mdash; one of the two equal shareholder-directors &mdash; filed a petition under Section 241 of the Companies Act, 2013 in October 2018, saying she had received no notice of any such meeting. The consideration itself was contested: the deed recorded Rs 4.36 crore, while the son&rsquo;s side asserted a larger agreed figure, part of which was routed through him.</p>

<h2>Where the tribunals went wrong</h2>
<p>The NCLT had faulted the purchaser for producing only a copy of the resolution and for inadequate due diligence, and treated the sale as void ab initio. The Supreme Court inverted that allocation of proof:</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Tribunals&rsquo; approach.</strong> The outsider must establish that the board meeting was validly convened and held; failing that, the deed falls and the purchaser&rsquo;s bona fides are irrelevant.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Supreme Court&rsquo;s approach.</strong> An unrelated third party has no means of knowing a company&rsquo;s internal affairs. Proof that a meeting was or was not held lies on the shareholders and directors; the purchaser may rely on the resolution produced to it.</div>
</div>

<h2>Conduct closed the door</h2>
<p>The judgment is as much about acquiescence as doctrine. The sale consideration flowed into the company&rsquo;s accounts and appeared in audited financial statements signed by the complaining director herself; the annual disclosure (Note 14) recorded the transaction. A petition filed over three years later, in apparent response to the purchaser&rsquo;s pending suit for specific performance, could not be allowed to plead ignorance. The Court also noted the company&rsquo;s refusal to produce income tax returns and drew an adverse inference. On that record, the tribunals erred in declaring the resolution and the deed null and void; the oppression petition itself was dismissed.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for transactions and tribunal practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For purchasers from companies.</strong> Obtain a certified copy of the board resolution and keep the money trail into the company&rsquo;s account clean &mdash; those two facts carried the day here.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For dissenting shareholders.</strong> Challenge a disputed transaction promptly. Signed audited accounts disclosing the sale, followed by years of silence, will be read as acquiescence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For Section 241 strategy.</strong> Oppression jurisdiction is not a collateral route to unwind a completed conveyance to an outsider; relief that strips a bona fide purchaser of title will need proof that the purchaser was on notice of the internal defect.</div>
</div>
<p>The decision will be cited wherever closely-held companies with deadlocked boards litigate over asset sales &mdash; a frequent pattern in NCLT petitions filed from Delhi NCR, where family-held companies hold substantial land assets.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
